@inject IConfiguration Configuration
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy Policy";
    var contactEmail = Configuration["Site:ContactEmail"];
}

<article class="container legal">
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="legal-updated">Last updated: 24 October 2026</p>

    <p>
        PhotoWorld is a community for people who love photography. This policy explains what personal
        information we collect when you use PhotoWorld, why, where it is kept and the choices you have. What you
        agree to when you use PhotoWorld, including that you keep the rights to what you share, is in our
        <a asp-action="Terms">Terms of Use</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What we collect</h2>
    <h3>When you sign in with Google or Apple</h3>
    <p>
        You join and sign in to PhotoWorld with your Google or Apple account. With your consent, the one you
        use shares with us:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>your name (Apple shares it only the first time, and you can edit it before sharing),</li>
        <li>your email address (only if it has been verified),</li>
        <li>your profile picture (Google only; Apple doesn't share one),</li>
        <li>a unique account identifier.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        With Apple you can choose <strong>Hide My Email</strong>: we then get a private relay address from Apple that
        forwards to you, and never see your real one.
    </p>
    <p>
        If your Google and Apple accounts share the same verified email address, they sign in to the same PhotoWorld account. Your name and
        picture on PhotoWorld are the ones from the account you signed in with most recently. For Apple, we also keep a
        token Apple gives us, used only to end PhotoWorld's access to your Apple account when you delete your account.
    </p>
    <p>
        We never see your Google or Apple password. We don't ask for access to your Gmail, contacts, Drive or
        other Google data, or your iCloud or other Apple data, and we never post anything on your behalf.
    </p>

    <h3>Account activity</h3>
    <p>We record when your account was created and when you last signed in.</p>

    <h3>Photo walks</h3>
    <p>
        When you organise a photo walk, its title, plan, time and meeting point (name and pin on the map) are shown to
        members, and your followers are told about it. When you join one, other members can see that you're going. Walks
        go with your account; members you blocked (or who blocked you) don't see your walks. Meeting other people is up to
        you: walks are organised by members, not by PhotoWorld.
    </p>

    <h3>Insights</h3>
    <p>
        When a signed-in member opens one of your photos, we add one to that photo's view count for the day. We don't
        record who viewed it, and your own views don't count. Only you can see your insights; daily counts go when the
        photo is deleted.
    </p>

    <h3>Feedback on photos</h3>
    <p>
        If you ask for feedback on a photo, the ratings and comments other members give are visible only to you (each
        giver also sees their own). Others only see how many members gave feedback. You can remove any of it.
    </p>

    <h3>Search and tags</h3>
    <p>
        Other members can find you by your name in search, and find your photos by their titles, descriptions and
        tags. Explore suggests members with photos to follow. Only what members could already see is ever shown.
    </p>

    <h3>Members you follow or block</h3>
    <p>
        We keep a list of the members you follow, so their photos and posts appear in your Following tabs.
        Members can see how many followers you have and how many members you follow, but not who they are.
        We also keep the list of members you block, which only you can see; blocked members aren't told.
    </p>

    <h3>Activity</h3>
    <p>
        To show you notifications (likes, comments, new followers, news from members you follow and photo challenges),
        we record what happened, who did it and when you read it, and which kinds you switched off. Notifications are
        deleted after 90 days.
    </p>

    <h3>Admins</h3>
    <p>
        A few PhotoWorld admins look after the community. To do that they can see members' names, email addresses,
        join and last sign-in dates and how much they've shared, and they can suspend accounts that break the
        <a asp-action="Terms">Terms of Use</a>. We record when an account was suspended and why.
    </p>

    <h3>Technical logs</h3>
    <p>
        To keep PhotoWorld running and secure, our servers write technical logs: for example, the page or API
        address requested, error details, and sign-in events with your PhotoWorld user ID. Logs don't contain
        your password or sign-in tokens.
    </p>

    <h3>Photos you share</h3>
    <p>
        A PhotoWorld admin looks at each photo you upload before it appears in the gallery; until it's approved only you
        and the admins can see it, and if it isn't approved, the admin's reason is shown only to you.
        Approved photos, with their titles, descriptions, likes and comments, are visible to other signed-in
        PhotoWorld members, unless you choose to feature a photo on the home page (see below). We read the camera settings stored in your photo (camera, lens, focal length,
        aperture, shutter speed, ISO and the date it was taken), show them on the photo page and let members
        search the gallery by them.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you enter a photo in a <strong>photo challenge</strong>, other members can see it in that challenge with
        its votes and place, and a win is shown on your member page.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you tick <strong>Show on the PhotoWorld home page</strong> for a photo, that photo, its title and your
        name appear in the slideshow on our public home page, where anyone can see them without signing in.
        It's off by default and you can turn it off at any time by editing the photo.
    </p>
    <p>
        We never store your original file. We save a resized copy with all hidden metadata removed, so the
        location where a photo was taken is never kept or shared. The image files are served from a public
        address: anyone who is given the direct link to an image can view it, even without signing in.
    </p>

    <h3>Your public portfolio</h3>
    <p>
        If you switch on <strong>My portfolio</strong>, anyone with its address (<code>/p/your-name</code>) can see it,
        without signing in: your name and profile picture, the photos you choose for it with their titles and
        descriptions, and the bio, website, Instagram name and contact email you fill in. Search engines and link previews
        may pick it up. It's off until you switch it on, you can turn it off at any time, and photos hidden by moderation
        or waiting for approval never appear on it.
    </p>

    <h3>Before and after</h3>
    <p>
        If you add an unedited version of a photo, it's shown wherever the photo is, with a slider to compare. It's
        processed like any upload (location and camera data removed) and deleted with the photo.
    </p>

    <h3>Golden hour</h3>
    <p>
        Sunrise, sunset and golden-hour times for photo walks and photo places are worked out on our server or your
        device from the place's position. Nothing is sent to anyone else.
    </p>

    <h3>Collections</h3>
    <p>
        Collections you make, and which photos you save to them, are private unless you make a collection public, in
        which case other members can see it on your member page. Saving someone's photo doesn't tell them.
    </p>

    <h3>Blog posts and comments</h3>
    <p>
        Blog posts you publish, and comments you write on posts, are visible to other signed-in members.
        Drafts are visible only to you. When you submit a new post, a PhotoWorld admin reads it before it's published;
        until then only you and the admins can see it, and if it isn't approved, the admin's reason is shown only to
        you. Photos in your posts come from your own gallery.
    </p>

    <h3>Places you've been</h3>
    <p>
        On <strong>My places</strong> you can pin places you've visited, each with a name, an optional date and
        optional notes. You add them yourself: we never take them from your photos. Your places are
        <strong>private</strong>: only you can see them, and they don't appear on your profile, your blog or anywhere
        else on PhotoWorld, with one exception you choose: if you say where one of your photos was taken, that
        place's <strong>name and pin</strong> are shown with the photo and on the <strong>Spots</strong> map to
        members who can see the photo. Its notes and date stay private, and so do your other places. Remove the
        place from the photo (or delete the photo or the place) to take it off the map.
    </p>
    <p>
        The map is drawn by <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org" rel="noopener">OpenStreetMap</a>, so your browser
        loads map images from their servers, which see your IP address and the area you're viewing. When you search
        for a place, or click the map and we suggest a name, what you typed or the point you clicked is sent to
        OpenStreetMap's <a href="https://nominatim.org" rel="noopener">Nominatim</a> service. Nothing about your
        account is sent. See the <a href="https://osmfoundation.org/wiki/Privacy_Policy" rel="noopener">OpenStreetMap
        Foundation privacy policy</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        In the PhotoWorld app for iPhone and iPad, the map is Apple Maps instead, and place searches and suggested
        names use Apple's location services, under <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/" rel="noopener">Apple's
        privacy policy</a>. In the Android app they are provided by Google Maps and Android's location services, under
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener">Google's privacy policy</a>. The apps only use
        the camera or your photo library when you choose to share a photo, and only the photo you pick is uploaded.
    </p>

    <h3>Email notifications</h3>
    <p>
        If you switch on <strong>email notifications</strong> in your account settings (or the app), we store the email
        address you choose, how often you want emails (as soon as a post is published, daily or weekly) and when we last
        emailed you. If it isn't the verified address you signed in with, we first send it a confirmation link
        and email nothing else until you click it. We use it only to tell you about new blog posts on PhotoWorld and, if
        you switch on the <strong>weekly summary</strong>, to send you once a week the number of views, likes, comments,
        feedback and new followers your photos got, and photo walks coming up from people you follow. Every
        email has an unsubscribe link, and you can switch notifications off or change the address at any time. The
        emails are delivered by our email service provider, which processes the address and the email only to send it.
    </p>

    <h2>How we use it</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>to create your account and recognise you when you sign in,</li>
        <li>to show your name and picture on your profile and next to what you share,</li>
        <li>to show your photos, likes and comments to other members,</li>
        <li>to keep the service secure, investigate problems and prevent abuse.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We don't sell your personal information, and we don't use it for advertising.</p>

    <h2 id="cookies">Cookies</h2>
    <p>PhotoWorld only uses cookies that are necessary for the site to work:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>PhotoWorld.Auth</strong>: keeps you signed in (up to 8 hours).</li>
        <li><strong>PhotoWorld.External</strong>: used for a few minutes while you sign in with Google or Apple.</li>
        <li><strong>.AspNetCore.Antiforgery</strong> and <strong>.AspNetCore.Correlation</strong>: protect forms
            and the sign-in process against forgery.</li>
        <li><strong>.AspNetCore.Mvc.CookieTempDataProvider</strong>: carries one-time messages, such as the
            welcome message after you sign in, to the next page.</li>
        <li><strong>PhotoWorld.Consent</strong>: remembers your cookie choice, for a year.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        If you allow <strong>preferences</strong>, we also remember your light, dark or automatic theme in your browser's
        storage (<code>pw-theme</code>). If you don't, it lasts until you close the browser. Change this any time with
        <strong>Cookie settings</strong> at the bottom of every page.
    </p>
    <p>We don't use analytics or advertising cookies.</p>

    <h2>Where your data is stored</h2>
    <p>
        Your account information, photos and our logs are stored by
        <a href="https://supabase.com" rel="noopener">Supabase</a> in the London (eu-west-2) region.
        Sign-in is provided by Google, under <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener">Google's
        privacy policy</a>, and by Apple, under <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/" rel="noopener">Apple's privacy
        policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Account information</strong>: for as long as you have a PhotoWorld account.</li>
        <li><strong>Portfolio settings</strong>: until you delete your account (switching the portfolio off hides it straight away).</li>
        <li><strong>Photos, blog posts, places, likes, comments, follows, blocks, collections and challenge entries</strong>: until you delete them or your account.</li>
        <li><strong>Notification email address and settings</strong>: until you delete your account. Switching
            notifications off stops all emails; you can also replace the address.</li>
        <li><strong>Technical logs</strong>: 30 days, then deleted automatically.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Your choices and rights</h2>
    <p>
        You can delete your account, with all your photos, places, notification settings, likes and comments, at any time from
        <strong>Account settings</strong> on your member page. It takes effect immediately, and if you used Sign in with
        Apple we also end PhotoWorld's access to your Apple account. You can also ask us
        for a copy of the personal information we hold about you, or ask us to correct it. You can also withdraw PhotoWorld's access at any
        time from your <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/connections" rel="noopener">Google account
        settings</a> or your <a href="https://account.apple.com" rel="noopener">Apple Account</a> (Sign in with Apple). If you live in the UK or EU, you can also complain to your data protection authority.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>PhotoWorld is not intended for children under 13, and we don't knowingly collect their information.</p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>If we change this policy, we'll update the date at the top of this page.</p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(contactEmail))
    {
        <p>Questions or requests about your data: <a href="mailto:@contactEmail">@contactEmail</a>.</p>
    }
    else
    {
        <p class="alert alert-warning">Set <code>Site:ContactEmail</code> in configuration to show a contact address here.</p>
    }
</article>
